@* A broken rule, printed under the row that broke it.

   **Visible, never hover-only.** The rows already carry a term-description tip from the
   hover-description work, and a finding is tempting to fold into the same plumbing — but WCAG
   is explicit that information carried only by a tooltip is information some readers do not
   get, and a rule the character has broken is exactly that kind of information. This renders in
   the ordinary flow of the page, nothing to hover and nothing to dismiss, whenever the engine
   has something to say about this row. It is never the only place a finding is said, either —
   the GM review step still prints every one, in full, with the budget findings this component
   never sees.

   **The engine decides; this only prints what SheetFindings already routed.** Nothing here
   prices, validates, or decides which row an issue belongs to — that is SheetFindings' job,
   reading the SubjectKind/SubjectId/OwnerId CharacterValidator already filled in. This component
   receives a list already meant for it and draws it.

   **Error and warning are never colour alone.** A printed word leads every sentence, and the
   left edge differs in *style* as well as in colour — solid against dashed — because a colour
   difference alone fails WCAG 1.4.1 for the one reader in twelve who cannot use it. *@

@if (Issues is { Count: > 0 })
{
    <ul class="row-findings">
        @foreach (var issue in Issues)
        {
            <li class="finding @Kind(issue)">
                <span class="finding-kind">@Label(issue)</span>
                @issue.Message
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The findings that belong to this row, already routed by <see cref="SheetFindings"/>.
    /// Null or empty draws nothing — most rows break no rule, and a row that is fine should look
    /// exactly like one nobody has looked at yet.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<ValidationIssue>? Issues { get; set; }

    // Kept inside @code rather than written as `issue.Severity == ValidationSeverity.Error`
    // in the markup above: NoPageNamesATypeThisProjectDeclares strips the @code block before
    // it scans for a compound type name, and does not strip an ordinary C# statement sitting
    // inside a @foreach in the templated region — a type name written there prints on the
    // player's screen as far as that guard can tell.
    private static bool IsError(ValidationIssue issue) => issue.Severity == ValidationSeverity.Error;

    private static string Kind(ValidationIssue issue) => IsError(issue) ? "error" : "warning";

    private static string Label(ValidationIssue issue) => IsError(issue) ? "Error" : "Warning";
}
